                                    ------
                                    Extension Usage
                                    ------
                                    Jay Liang
                                    ------
                                     2007/09/08

RSS Extension Elements 
	
	If you need to write programs to read or write RSS feeds, you will know that the RSS formats are very
extensible. You can pretty much add any additional elements to the feeds. There are many other XML formats, such
as format that Google Base uses and Microsoft's simple sharing format, that are based on the basic RSS format and
adds new functionality by adding extension elements. 

	 An engineer from Google wrote an MapReduce program to go through all the feeds that Google Reader keeps track of
and got the top 50 extension namespaces that are being used (see more at {{http://googlereader.blogspot.com/2006/08/namespaced-extensions-in-feeds.html}}).
Currently Yarfraw supports quite a few of them. I am also using this list to prioritize extension support to be added in the
future.

*----------+--------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+
|| <<% of Feeds>> || <<Namespace>>    || <<URI>> || Supported by Yarfraw ||       
*----------+--------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+
|29.36%  	|{{{http://dublincore.org/documents/dces/}Dublin Core}}  	|http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/| Yes|
*----------+--------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+
|15.71%  	|{{{http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml}XHTML}}  	|http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml | Partial, there are some util methods for working with xhtml elements |
*----------+--------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+
|11.92%  	|{{{http://code.blogger.com/archives/atom-docs.html#extensions}Blogger Atom API Extensions}}  	|http://www.blogger.com/atom/ns# | Yes|
*----------+--------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+
|11.88%  	|{{{http://code.blogger.com/archives/atom-docs.html#draft}Blogger Draft Extension}}  	|http://purl.org/atom-blog/ns# | Yes|
*----------+--------------+----------------+----------------+----------------+
|11.16%  	|{{{http://web.resource.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/}RSS 1.0 Content Module}}  	|http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/| Partial, the most popular element \<encoded\> is supported by the core model directly|
